# The develop chain, as a shared fragment.
#
# Included rather than copied, which is the point of it: the same tail was
# hand-written in hdr.tgl, panorama.tgl and autodevelop.tgl, and had already
# drifted -- hdr had six stages, the other two had one. Changing the order of
# denoise and tone mapping meant editing three files and noticing the third.
#
#   include "_develop.tgl"
#
# CONTRACT, and it is a convention rather than something the language enforces:
#
#   in   `developed` must already hold the image to develop.
#   out  `developed` is left holding the developed image.
#
# Named for both so the fragment reads as a transformation of one variable, and
# so a caller can include it twice in a chain if it ever wants to.
#
# The leading underscore marks it as a fragment rather than a script worth
# opening: it calls no image() and no display(), so on its own it does nothing.
#
# WHY THIS ORDER, since it is the part that is easy to get wrong and the reason
# having one copy matters:
#
#   tonemap        first, because everything after it wants display-referred
#                  values. A scene-linear merge can reach 900 with the midtones
#                  near 2.0, and no adjustment below is meaningful on that
#                  scale.
#
#   basic_adjust   the ordinary exposure/contrast/colour controls, fused into
#                  one GPU pass rather than chained -- at 36 MP each separate
#                  stage would read and write a 144 MB intermediate.
#
#   dehaze         before the local contrast and grain, because it inverts a
#                  physical mixing of light and wants the picture still clean.
#
#   wavelet_denoise  after the tone curve, NOT before it. A shrinkage threshold
#                  is a fixed distance in the value domain, so ahead of a tone
#                  curve it thresholds in linear scene space where shadow
#                  detail is compressed into almost nothing -- it would erase
#                  exactly what it is meant to preserve.
#
#   orton          a creative effect, so it goes after the corrections.
#
#   vignette       last, because it is a property of the lens rather than of
#                  the scene: darkening applied before the tone curve would be
#                  partly undone by it.
#
# Every stage is params(), so each contributes its own group of sliders.
#
# THE EFFECTS START SWITCHED OFF -- dehaze, orton and vignette here, plus bloom
# and film_grain wherever they appear. That is what makes this fragment safe to
# include: a script gains the controls without gaining a glow and a vignette on
# every photograph it touches. Tick a stage's `enabled` box to turn it on; its
# group is collapsed until you do, so the panel shows what is actually acting
# on the picture rather than a wall of controls that are not.
#
# The corrections -- tonemap, basic_adjust, wavelet_denoise -- stay on, because
# a develop chain that does not develop is not useful.
#
# A switched-off stage costs nothing at all: it is skipped entirely, with no
# allocation, no dispatch and no copy, and passes its input straight through.

developed = developed
         => params( basic_adjust, auto_exposure = 1 )()
         => params( dehaze )()
         => params( wavelet_denoise )()
         => params( orton )()
         => params( vignette )()
